    "a": {
      "text": "Right. Three months of data, 155 transactions, €23,665 tracked. Here's what stands out.\n\n---\n\n**Housing is running the show.**\n\nAt 48% of total spend — €4,200 — it's the single largest claim on your money by a distance. That's not unusual in itself, but it matters specifically for your retirement pot goal. Every euro locked into housing costs is a euro that isn't compounding over 15 years. The concentration is worth keeping an eye on.\n\n---\n\n**This month is a different animal.**\n\nSpending hit €13,315 against €4,156 the previous month — a 220% jump. The biggest single driver is uncategorised spend, which means there's a chunk of that number that hasn't been labelled yet. Until it's sorted, the real shape of this month is still blurry. Worth cleaning that up before drawing conclusions.\n\n[STATS]\nTracked spend | €23,665\nhousing | 48%\nRecurring bills | 23\n[/STATS]\n\n---\n\n**23 recurring bills. €5,950 a month. 45% of average monthly spend.**\n\nThat's a significant structural layer — and it's the kind of thing that quietly accumulates over time. Some of those will be firmly Foundation (mortgage, utilities, insurance). But in 23 bills there's almost always overlap or dead weight. A single audit pass on that list usually finds one or two that have outlived their purpose — and anything redirected from there goes straight toward the retirement pot without touching your day-to-day behaviour at all.\n\n---\n\n**One structural fix worth trying:** move a fixed amount toward your retirement pot on payday, before anything else moves. The experiment is simple — agree the amount, transfer it on payday, don't pull it back. 30 days. Pay-yourself-first beats willpower every time.\n\n[OPTIONS]\n- Yes, let's try it\n- Pick a different one\n- Break down my housing\n[/OPTIONS]\n\n— C.",
